// std::any — a value whose type is only known at run time.
//
// `Any` is a builtin type, so nothing here has to be imported: the methods
// below are inherent to it and are in scope wherever an `Any` is.
//
//     let boxed: Any = 42            // anything at all converts, implicitly
//     boxed.typeName()               // "i64"
//     boxed is i64                   // true
//     boxed.get::<i64>()             // Some(42)
//     boxed.get::<String>()          // nil
//     boxed.expect::<i64>()          // 42, and aborts if it were not one
//
// How it is represented
// ---------------------
// An `Any` is one pointer: the value itself when it is a class, and a
// reference-counted box around it otherwise. Either way the object header in
// front of it names the type, and that name is what every question here is
// answered from — so the answer comes from the value, never from a promise
// the program made about it.
//
// Because the descriptor a type gets is one symbol across the whole program,
// `is`, `holds` and `get` are a pointer comparison in the ordinary case.
//
// What it costs
// -------------
// Boxing allocates, once, when the value goes in — except for a class, which
// is already an object and is only retained. Coming back out copies the value
// and retains whatever it holds. A `Unique<T>` cannot go in at all: an `Any`
// takes a reference of its own, and a `Unique` has room for one owner.
//
// Reaching for `Any` is worth a second thought. When the set of types is
// closed, an enum says so and `match` makes the compiler check you covered
// it. When they share behaviour, `dyn Mark` dispatches without asking what
// the type is. `Any` is for the case where neither is true — a heterogeneous
// container, a value crossing a boundary that has no type to speak of — and
// where the check therefore has to happen at run time.

extend Any {
    /// The fully qualified name of the type the value actually has:
    /// `"i64"`, `"String"`, `"app::Point"`, `"[3:f64]"`.
    ///
    /// Two types share this name only if they are the same type — the module
    /// path and any generic arguments are part of it.
    #intrinsic("any_type_name")
    pub fn typeName(&self) -> String

    /// True when the value inside is a `T`.
    ///
    /// For a class this is the `is` question, so a `Dog` also holds as an
    /// `Animal`. For everything else it is exact: a `u32` does not hold as an
    /// `i64`, even though one converts to the other.
    ///
    ///     if boxed.holds::<String>() { ... }
    ///
    /// `boxed is String` is the same test written as an operator.
    #intrinsic("any_holds")
    pub fn holds<T>(&self) -> bool

    /// The value as a `T`, or `nil` when it is something else. This is the
    /// one to reach for: the test and the extraction happen together, so
    /// there is no way to read the value as a type it does not have.
    ///
    ///     match boxed.get::<i64>() {
    ///         Option::Some(n) => io::println(n * 2),
    ///         Option::None => io::println("not a number"),
    ///     }
    ///
    ///     let n = boxed.get::<i64>() ?? 0
    #intrinsic("any_get")
    pub fn get<T>(&self) -> T?

    /// The value as a `T`. Aborts when it is something else, naming both the
    /// type that was there and the one that was wanted.
    ///
    /// Use it where the type is an invariant of the program rather than
    /// something to be checked; use `get` everywhere else.
    #intrinsic("any_expect")
    pub fn expect<T>(&self) -> T
}
